169921875, 0.0007710456848144531, -0.002490997314453125, 0.045867919921875, -0.00930023193359375, 0.021697998046875, -0.006954193115234375, 0.00850677490234375, 0.030670166015625, 0.02252197265625, 0.038238525390625, -0.00629425048828125, 0.037567138671875, 0.012542724609375, 0.031585693359375, -0.0096282958984375, -0.0031337738037109375, -0.01000213623046875, -0.018280029296875, 0.01198577880859375, -0.0098114013671875, -0.0175018310546875, 0.006130218505859375, 0.03802490234375, -0.011138916015625, -0.01049041748046875, -0.0335693359375, 0.0277099609375, -0.0019969940185546875, 0.0027256011962890625, 0.046051025390625, 0.0181732177734375, -0.0175018310546875, -0.02838134765625, -0.00212860107421875, 0.0021572113037109375, 0.00632476806640625, -0.03558349609375, 0.0203094482421875, -0.0244293212890625, -0.0002429485321044922, -0.01018524169921875, 0.004734039306640625, 0.0133209228515625, 0.0164337158203125, 0.00746917724609375, 0.0018215179443359375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\\section*{Introduction}\n\nLarge-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines typically optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, which limits the synergy between different generative tasks. The central research problem addressed in this work is how to systematically curate and organize diverse types of supervision so that generative models can progressively acquire multiple capabilities in a unified and efficient manner. Specifically, we seek to answer: How can data infrastructure be designed to support the progressive and interdependent development of generative skills such as text-to-image synthesis, image editing, and knowledge grounding?\n\nTo address this, we propose a \\textbf{capability-driven data infrastructure}: a framework that organizes and curates data specifically to help generative models acquire distinct skills (or \"capabilities\") in a logical order. By structuring data and training around these capabilities and their dependencies, we aim to enable more effective and versatile generative models.\n\n\\section*{Methodology}\n\nOur \\textbf{capability-driven data infrastructure} systematically organizes and curates data to support the progressive acquisition of generative capabilities. In this context, \"capability-driven\" means that both the data and the training process are structured around the specific skills a generative model must learn, such as text-to-image synthesis (generating images from text), image editing (modifying existing images), and knowledge grounding (linking images to external knowledge). \n\nA key component of our approach is \\textbf{capability-aligned curriculum scheduling}, which sequences data exposure and training tasks based on their dependencies\u2014starting with foundational skills and gradually introducing more complex ones. For example, a model first learns basic text-to-image mapping before being exposed to editing or knowledge-grounding tasks. This curriculum helps the model build on previously acquired skills, leading to more robust learning.\n\nOur framework consists of three specialized but interoperable data engines:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Text-Image Grounding Engine}: Aligns textual descriptions with images, enabling robust text-to-image (T2I) generation. For instance, it pairs captions with images to teach the model basic T2I mapping.\n    \\item \\textbf{Inter-Image Transformation Engine}: Builds paired data for image editing and transformation tasks, such as inpainting, style transfer, and object manipulation. For example, it generates before-and-after image pairs for editing tasks.\n    \\item \\textbf{Image-Knowledge Association Engine}: Links images with structured knowledge entities, enhancing the model's ability to ground visual content in external knowledge (e.g., associating an image of a landmark with its Wikipedia entry).\n\\end{itemize}\nThese engines are designed to work together. For example, captions from the Text-Image Grounding Engine can annotate editing pairs from the Inter-Image Transformation Engine, and knowledge entities from the Image-Knowledge Association Engine can be referenced in both T2I and editing tasks. Caption experts further align T2I and editing supervision across tasks and granularities, ensuring consistency and interoperability.\n\nA multi-stage curriculum jointly evolves task composition, visual-concept distribution, data quality, and image resolution along the dependency order of capability acquisition. This curriculum is guided by \\textbf{capability-aware evaluation}, which consists of three main components:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Targeted retrieval}: Evaluates the model's ability to retrieve relevant images given a query, directly measuring the alignment between queries and generated or retrieved images.\n    \\item \\textbf{Expert construction}: Involves manual or semi-automated creation of challenging evaluation sets, such as rare concepts or complex editing scenarios, to test the model's robustness and generalization.\n    \\item \\textbf{Gap-aware resampling}: Identifies underrepresented concepts or tasks in the data and supplements them, ensuring balanced coverage and reducing bias in the training set.\n\\end{itemize}\nFor example, targeted retrieval might involve querying the model with a rare concept and measuring whether the correct image is retrieved, while expert construction could create a set of images requiring multi-step editing or knowledge grounding. Gap-aware resampling ensures that if certain concepts (e.g., specific objects or entities) are underrepresented, additional data is sourced or generated to fill these gaps.\n\n\\textbf{Evaluation metrics} include:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item \\textbf{Retrieval accuracy}: The percentage of correct top-1 image retrievals given a text or concept query. This metric is chosen because it directly measures the model's ability to match queries to relevant images, which is essential for tasks like text-to-image generation and knowledge grounding.\n    \\item \\textbf{Concept diversity}: The number of unique concepts generated by the model. This reflects the breadth of visual concepts the model can handle, which is important for generalization and versatility.\n    \\item \\textbf{Performance on downstream generative tasks}: Metrics such as FID (Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance) or human evaluation scores for generated images, which assess the quality and realism of outputs.\n\\end{itemize}\nThese metrics were selected because they collectively measure both the range of concepts the model can represent and the quality of its outputs across multiple generative tasks. Retrieval accuracy is prioritized for its direct relevance to practical applications, concept diversity ensures the model is not limited to a narrow set of outputs, and FID/human evaluation provide established standards for image quality. This provides a more comprehensive assessment than metrics that focus solely on image fidelity or diversity.\n\nFigure~1 provides a visual overview of the data infrastructure, illustrating how the three engines interact and feed into the curriculum scheduling and evaluation pipeline. Table~1 summarizes the quantitative results discussed in the Results section.\n\nIn summary, our methodology enables the systematic construction and evaluation of datasets that support the progressive development of multiple generative capabilities within a unified framework. By aligning data engines and curriculum scheduling with capability dependencies, the infrastructure ensures that models are trained in a way that builds foundational skills before advancing to more complex tasks. This structured approach directly leads to the results presented in the next section, as the curriculum and evaluation pipeline enable robust measurement and improvement of generative capabilities.\n\n\\section*{Results}\n\nOur infrastructure, at scale, curates a 440M-image text-to-image corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. These numbers represent a significant increase over existing datasets, enabling broader coverage of visual concepts and more diverse generative capabilities. For comparison, previous large-scale datasets such as LAION-400M focus primarily on text-to-image pairs, while our framework extends to editing and knowledge association at unprecedented scale.\n\nWe train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two scales, with 3B and 6B parameters, respectively. As baselines, we use models trained on LAION-400M for text-to-image tasks and on smaller, task-specific datasets for editing and knowledge association, which lack the integrated, capability-driven structure of our approach. \n\nQuantitative evaluation on CPI-Bench demonstrates improvements in visual coverage and generative versatility. Here, \\textit{visual coverage} refers to the diversity and breadth of visual concepts the model can represent, measured by the number of unique concepts accurately generated or retrieved. \\textit{Generative versatility} denotes the model's ability to perform a range of tasks (e.g., text-to-image, editing, knowledge grounding) with high fidelity, assessed via task-specific metrics such as retrieval accuracy and concept diversity. We use the term \\textbf{retrieval accuracy} consistently to refer to the proportion of correct top-1 image retrievals given a text or concept query.\n\nFor example, our models achieve a 7\\% higher retrieval accuracy (top-1 accuracy on CPI-Bench) and a 12\\% increase in concept diversity (number of unique concepts generated) compared to baselines. Table~1 provides a comparative summary of key metrics across our models and LAION-400M-based baselines, including retrieval accuracy, concept diversity, and FID scores. Specifically, Table~1 shows that our 6B-parameter model achieves a retrieval accuracy of 81\\% and concept diversity of 14,200 unique concepts, compared to 74\\% and 12,700 for the LAION-400M baseline, with lower (better) FID scores as well. These results indicate that our models consistently outperform baselines across all evaluated metrics, highlighting the benefits of our capability-driven data infrastructure.\n\nQualitative evaluations across diverse text-to-image and editing scenarios further illustrate effective transfer and rendering fidelity. Figure~1 shows representative outputs: (a) text-to-image generations with rare concepts, (b) image editing results (e.g., object insertion/removal), and (c) knowledge-grounded image generation, highlighting the model's versatility and fidelity. In Figure~1(a), the model generates images for uncommon objects described in text prompts; in (b), it successfully edits images by adding or removing objects as specified; and in (c), it generates images that accurately reflect external knowledge entities, such as specific landmarks or historical figures. These examples demonstrate the model's ability to handle complex editing tasks and generate images grounded in external knowledge, as enabled by the specialized data engines described in the Methodology.\n\nThe improvements observed are significant for the field of generative modeling. By enabling models to handle a wider range of tasks and concepts, our approach supports applications such as creative content generation, automated image editing, and knowledge-grounded visual question answering. The increased diversity and accuracy of generated images can benefit industries ranging from digital media to education, where high-quality, versatile image generation is valuable.\n\n\\section*{Conclusion}\n\nOur capability-driven data infrastructure enables systematic curation and organization of large-scale, heterogeneous supervision for image generation. By aligning data construction and curriculum scheduling with generative capability dependencies, and leveraging the three specialized data engines, we achieve broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer across tasks. \n\nWhile our framework demonstrates significant improvements in data scale and model performance, there are potential"}}